11114501953125, -0.005733489990234375, -0.00384521484375, -0.005718231201171875, 0.0118865966796875, 0.00516510009765625, 0.0129852294921875, -0.061370849609375, 0.00586700439453125, -0.0031890869140625, -0.0015506744384765625, 0.0133819580078125, 0.01296234130859375, 0.0041961669921875, -0.0200958251953125, 0.00687408447265625, -0.00363922119140625, -0.00359344482421875, 0.012115478515625, -0.025848388671875, -0.044830322265625, -0.0034313201904296875, 0.00327301025390625, -0.0008955001831054688, 0.00586700439453125, 0.0011396408081054688, -0.0169677734375, 0.008209228515625, -0.0311737060546875, -0.0192718505859375, -0.0299835205078125, 0.01216888427734375, 0.01342010498046875, -0.025054931640625, 0.0053558349609375, 0.02667236328125, -0.013336181640625, 0.01001739501953125, -0.00023353099822998047, -0.005588531494140625, 0.0153350830078125, -0.0010557174682617188, 0.038421630859375, -0.00662994384765625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "The emergence of organized spatiotemporal patterns is a common feature of oscillatory systems, ranging from neural populations to engineered networks. However, identifying these patterns and tracking their evolution over time is challenging, especially when systems display transient dynamics. In this work, we present a framework based on spatial ordinal patterns to characterize the spatiotemporal dynamics of oscillatory systems. Our method operates directly on the phase, rather than the amplitude, and introduces additional patterns to account for nearly equal phases. This symbolic representation encodes local spatial ordering relationships, capturing both phase gradients and synchronized clusters within a unified framework. Building on this representation, we define a spatial permutation entropy that quantifies the diversity of spatiotemporal patterns at each moment, enabling the detection of transient dynamics and regime transitions as they occur. We demonstrate that this approach distinguishes phase-locked states with identical global synchronization levels but different spatial organizations, and also characterizes partially synchronized states. We validate the method on synthetic oscillator networks across various spatiotemporal regimes, as well as on resting-state EEG recordings from human volunteers, where it differentiates between conditions within individual subjects."}}